Okay now....cab driver Robert Chobert said that the shooter was "about
200 lbs", 30lbs heavier than Abu-Jamal, not an unlikely mistake; many
people are not good at estimating weight.  Chobert also said he saw
Abu-Jamal shoot Faulkner, run a short distance before slumping to the
ground and being put in the police wagon where he was positively
identified by Chobert.  The other witnesses who claimed to have seen
someone running from the scene did not see anything until a short time
after the shootings.  Seeing anyone running at that point is pretty
inconclusive. 
And PUH-lease get this stuff straight if you are going to be so
supportive of Mumia....it was NOT a .44 caliber bullet that was fired
into Officer Faulkner.  An Assistant Medical Examiner who autopsied
Faulkner said that the bullets found in him were probably a .44
caliber.  The ballistics expert at the trial, who probably knows a bit
more about such things than an M.E., said that the bullets that killed
Officer Faulkner were definately from a .38 caliber gun.  The bullets
were of a high-velocity, extremely powerful type called "Plus-P" and
that bullet exploded on entering Faulkner's body, making a direct match
with any gun impossible.  But the ballistics expert also testified that
the markings on the bullets were consistent with Abu-Jamal's gun and
that the bullet casings found IN HIS GUN were of the same "Plus-P" make
as those found in Faulkner.  A fired .38 case measures .357 in
diameter.  A fired .44 measures .431 in diameter.  Not a big difference
to the naked eye but a very big difference to the ballistics
measurements.

