YES!  It is a MOST important detail!!!  A rogue armed cop breaking
into someone's house to burgle it is a thief, and killing him/her is
justifiable self-defense, NOT ANY CRIME AT ALL, in just about any
jurisdiction that bases its laws on Common Law.  Here the cop-killer
IS A HERO in my book.

A cop who fails to identify himself, does an act that would be
illegal were he not a cop, and provokes a reaction that kills him
could lead to the killer's act being ruled self-defense, involuntary
manslaughter (the defendant acted in self-defense, but used
excessive force, resulting in death), voluntary manslaughter (same
as before, but the defendant knew that the force used was
excessive), or murder, DEPENDING ON THE CIRCUMSTANCES and the
defendant's MENTAL STATE at the time.

The spouse who, in the heat of a lovers' quarrel grabs his/her
cop-husband's/wife's service revolver and shoots him/her would
probably be guilty of voluntary manslaughter, or unpremeditated
murder.

Conviction for premeditated murder (first-degree murder in some
jurisdictions) requires that the defendant have planned the murder
(or a criminal act that led to the murder--this covers armed robbers
who "accidentally" shoot their victims or innocent bystanders) in
advance, and not act in the heat of passion.

American society recognizes that these acts vary in seriousness and
imposes varying penalties, and I believe the law is correct in this
regard.  I, for one am very happy that society is able to
distinguish and make distinctions among this whole spectrum of
killings, and that it reserves its most extreme penalty for only the
gravest of acts.  And this is true regardless of whether the victim
is a cop, or an ordinary person-on-the-street.

(PS:  If you are planning a crime, don't take this as gospel.  I am
not a lawyer, and this is not legal advice.)

