Pardon my jumping in here, but I felt like I had to.  <g>

A PGP signature can not be forged unless the secret key is compromised, period.
The big weakness with public key systems is the 'man-in-the-middle attack" --
where someone substitutes their key for a legitimate key.  Remember, though,
that PGP has the ability to assign trust to a key, through the use of
signatures.

As an example: suppose I am 'FBI Agent Zeanah' (in reality I'm just College Kid
Zeanah).  Anyone can claim to be me, and in fact there might be 20 keys floating
around that all claim to be mine.  The ID on the key offers no guarantee that
the key actually belongs to me (look at the number of president@whitehouse.gov
keys floating around).  However, if Janet Reno had a PGP key that was available
to the public (on gov't key server, available via web page, or published on
CNN), then the situation changes.

Imagine that Reno signed the PGP keys of all of her agents.  In this scenario,
anyone adding my key to their keyring would see that Janet Reno was vouching for
the authenticity of my key; to impersonate me, someone would have to forge her
signature, and that would be a -very- difficult task. 

I wouldn't even need Janet Reno's signature -- anyone receiving my public key
could call their local FBI office, have them search their records, and announce
the 'fingerprint' for my public key:  8A 3F A5 1B 2B EF B6 8E  E6 94 7C 47 4F D0
D3 BE.  If the fingerprint matches, then all is well.  <g>

Of course, someone could go all out and have FBI phone calls re-routed (it's
happened to the FBI before), but then you're getting into some heavy-duty
forgery, and weaknesses in PGP are not at fault.

>I submit that I have a "chip on my shoulder" for people who makes
>ludicrous claims about any piece of software and who refuse to admit the
>limitations in that software. 

You're right -- PGP has limitations that -need- to be understood.

At the same time, however, PGP seems to offer the best system for protecting
privacy and certifying authenticity.  If nothing else, it's a hell of a lot more
secure than using no protection at all.  It's embarrassingly easy to forge
messages; PGP at least makes it harder.

Got a better system?



