> This is not censorship, this is editorship. Only the government can
> censor, and NPR is not the government. NPR has the right to produce and
> air what is wishes.

Private people or agencies have the right to publish or
broadcast, or refrain from publishing or broadcasting, as
they please. To the extent that NPR is private, it did not
censor.

But editorial decisions presume professional standards and
ethics. If an editor rejects an article for redundancy or
lack of research, she has made a professional decision. But
if the editor says, "We've had our quota of articles by
women or homosexuals this year, so this one has to go," that
is not a professional editorial decision. That is tantamount
to censorship, even though it may not technically be
censorship.

NPR's canceling Mumia Abu-Jamal was the moral equivalent of
censorship. As NPR reacted to pressure from government
agents -- police and prosecutors -- its decision hardly
differed from censorship.

