Mark Simpson (simplaw@netaxs.com) wrote:

: Are you saying that the University has actually taken this postion 
: against a student?  I find that very hard to believe - I would love to 
: see this alleged contract.

You'd better believe it.

Myself and another student wrote a 200 page astronomy lab manual for a 
class in technical writing. The project turned out so well that we 
decided to polish it for use in the course in which we were both TAs. 
After we finished the manual, and the professor who oversees the lab 
found it to his liking, we started using it. 

Both myself and my co-author have this thing about an instructor getting 
royalties from a book when that book is written by the person teaching 
the course, so we opted not to add royalties to the students price. We did 
want to receive compensation for its use, though. We negotiated with the 
department of physics and they agreed to pay us $375 dollars for its use. 
Not once did the subject of copyright transfer come up. Indeed, we made 
it explicit that we intended to attempt to sell the work to other 
schools. They had no problem with that.

The book was used for four semesters and two summer sessions. It had our 
names on the cover as authors and indicated the fact that it is a 
copyrighted work.

After we stopped teaching the course, we wanted to begin receiving 
royalties if the department continued to use the manual. They not only 
refused to pay royalties, but they also removed our names as authors from 
the book and changed the copyright notice to indicate that the dept. of 
physics was the copyright holder.

According to the legal counsel of the university, The university owns 
the copyright since we wrote the manual as part of a course for which we 
had received credit. Their position is not even considering anything 
that happened after that point. I would expect them to at least try to 
claim that the text was a work for hire since we were teaching the course 
(even though it wasn't) or try to claim that they had purchased copyright 
as a result of their payment for use; they haven't even raised those 
issues. They are using a university policy to steal our work.

I have consulted a local copyright attorney and he stated that their 
actions are a clear infringement of copyright, but that it would not be 
worth a suit since damages would be limited to the loss of royalties for 
that infringement (which at this point being summer is what would have 
been gained on the sale of 20 or so copies.) Apparently we have no 
recourse within the university either.

As students, we can not bring a formal action against the professor or 
the department through the faculty senate. Neither can we initiate an 
action in the student judicial court since the defendants would not be 
students. We are stuck having a contract which graduate students are 
required to sign (we were undergraduates when all this occurred and never 
even saw the contract) being shoved at us without recourse. 

Steve Crisp 
