Hypothetical:

Officer V is off duty.  D, unaware that V is a police officer, shoots without
premeditation in V's general direction.  The bullet hits V, causing a mortal
wound. 

This would be murder at common law, but under most statutes it would not be
first degree murder.  And the fact that D killed an officer is essentially
irrelevant on those facts.

Second hypothetical:

Officer V unjustifiably attacks D.  D subdues V, using lawful force to defend
himself.  D, enraged at the attack, then draws a gun and fatallay shoots V.

That would be manslaughter, because D was acting in "hot blood," within a
reasonable time of the provocation.

Would you consider these equivalent to a fully premeditated killing?

